WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.579
AI agents are more than chat interfaces.

2
00:00:04.659 --> 00:00:12.577
An agent may receive instructions, reason
over context, retrieve documents,

3
00:00:12.657 --> 00:00:21.295
read memory, call tools, ask for
approvals, and trigger actions in external systems.

4
00:00:21.375 --> 00:00:29.174
That makes monitoring different from
traditional application monitoring.

5
00:00:29.254 --> 00:00:34.893
You still care about errors, latency,
uptime, and cost,

6
00:00:34.973 --> 00:00:42.891
but you also need to understand what
information influenced the agent and what

7
00:00:42.971 --> 00:00:46.210
authority it used when it acted.

8
00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:52.649
Traditional logs often focus on request
and response details.

9
00:00:52.729 --> 00:00:58.847
Agent logs need to capture a broader
story: the user request,

10
00:00:58.927 --> 00:01:07.445
relevant system instructions, selected
context, model calls, retrieved sources,

11
00:01:07.525 --> 00:01:15.204
tool choices, tool arguments, policy
checks, approval decisions, errors,

12
00:01:15.284 --> 00:01:17.323
and final outcomes.

13
00:01:17.403 --> 00:01:25.202
Without that trace, teams may know that a
ticket changed or a message was sent,

14
00:01:25.282 --> 00:01:31.040
but not why the agent thought the action
was appropriate.

15
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:37.959
Logging supports security, reliability,
privacy, auditability,

16
00:01:38.039 --> 00:01:42.958
debugging, governance, and incident
response.

17
00:01:43.038 --> 00:01:48.796
Security teams need to investigate
suspicious actions.

18
00:01:48.876 --> 00:01:54.875
Engineering teams need to troubleshoot
failed tool calls.

19
00:01:54.955 --> 00:02:02.154
Privacy teams need to know what sensitive
data entered prompts or logs.

20
00:02:02.234 --> 00:02:10.632
Governance teams need evidence that
approvals and policy controls were followed.

21
00:02:10.712 --> 00:02:18.270
Users need confidence that agent behavior
can be explained after the fact.

22
00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:24.469
Monitoring must be designed before
production deployment.

23
00:02:24.549 --> 00:02:32.587
Retrofitting observability after an
incident usually leaves gaps at the worst

24
00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:34.467
possible moment.

25
00:02:34.547 --> 00:02:41.265
A strong design decides what events
matter, how to correlate them,

26
00:02:41.345 --> 00:02:49.263
what data must be redacted, who can access
logs, how long records are retained,

27
00:02:49.343 --> 00:02:53.782
and how logs connect to response
workflows.

28
00:02:53.862 --> 00:02:58.421
The goal is not to record everything
forever.

29
00:02:58.501 --> 00:03:04.500
The goal is to capture the right evidence
to operate safely.
